#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050044 :topic/difficulty 1.0599362345313645 :topic/id #uuid "283fdb1d-4be7-5644-aaf9-820e24ebcdcb" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183961 :question/id #uuid "fc7c982c-7e5d-5187-9779-6952f32fe2bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-15019" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3\n$$\n\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+7x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& ({x}^{2}+7x+\\frac{49}{4})+3 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183962 :question/id #uuid "05109436-7f8b-5ef9-a460-b53497a138f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15020" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{53}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183963 :question/id #uuid "2308b4bc-c09f-5f4c-b879-3bb67c92f885" :question/math-academy-id "e-15021" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\dfrac{49}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3842 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051078}] :topic/title "Completing the Square With Odd Linear Terms"}]}
